+Do I want more frames?            Off / 2x / 3x
+```
+
+Everything else — motion sources, depth conventions, HUD separation, optical-flow
+grids, bridge stages, Streamline tags, module load order, pacing, recovery — is the
+system's problem.
+
+That is not a request for fewer features. It is a request to move complexity from
+the person to the software, which is strictly harder than exposing it. This
+document is about how v3 carries that weight.
+
+---
+
+## What was wrong with v2
+
+v2 already believed all of the above. The user-facing design was right. What it
+lacked was a structure capable of keeping the promises the interface made.
+
+**The decision logic was 1,700 lines of mutable globals.** `ApplyGamingStyle`,
+`SetNeuralEnabled`, `ApplyNativeFrameGenPath`, `EnsureTemporalPolicy` and
+`ApplyCompatibilityMotionPath` each wrote configuration, set restart flags and
+mutated three shared integers. They called each other. In one path
+`EnsureTemporalPolicy` calls `ApplyCompatibilityMotionPath`, which sets
+`g_motion_path = 1`, and then the caller sets it back to `0`. The rules existed
+only as an emergent property of execution order. Nothing could be tested, and
+nothing could be explained to a user without re-deriving it.
+
+**The strength slider did not do what its label said.** The label read
+`0.00 = effect off | 1.00 = balanced | 2.00 = maximum`. The handler wrote the
+value into `g_nr.intensity` and nothing else. `global_tone`, `local_tone`,
+`local_structure` and `skin_structure` stayed at the look's fixed values at every
+slider position. At 0.00 the neural pass was still running, with four of its five
+parameters untouched.
+
+**ACTIVE could never go back down.** `RefreshBridgeRuntimeSnapshot` searched the
+bridge's log for `"frames delivered so far."`. Once that sentence had been written
+to the file it was there for the rest of the session, so the badge stayed green
+whether or not anything was still being delivered. The project's own stated
+principle —
+
+> ACTIVE bedeutet wirklich, dass Frames durch den betreffenden Pfad verarbeitet
+> werden.
+
+— was the exact thing the implementation could not do.
+
+**There was one source of truth, and it belonged to someone else.** Every claim
+about the neural path came from parsing a third-party log in a format we do not
+control.
+
+**Installation asked the user the hardest question first.** Two `.bat` files,
+`RP Visual` and `Full Neural`, presented before the player had any way to know
+which one their machine could run.
+
+v3 changes the structure, not the philosophy.
+
+---
+
+## The shape
+
+```
+        ┌─────────┐        ┌──────────────┐        ┌──────────┐
+ world →│ Probes  │──────→ │  Capability  │ ─────→ │ Planner  │
+        └─────────┘evidence│    graph     │snapshot└──────────┘
+                           └──────────────┘              │ plan
+                                  ▲                      ▼
+                                  │              ┌──────────────┐
+                                  └──────────────│  Effectors   │→ world
+                                     evidence    └──────────────┘
+                                                        │
+                                                        ▼
+                                                 ┌──────────────┐
+                                                 │  Status/UI   │
+                                                 └──────────────┘
+```
+
+Arrows point one way. Observation flows up, decisions flow down, and nothing in
+the middle reaches sideways. A probe cannot decide; an effector cannot decide;
+the interface cannot decide. There is exactly one function in the product that
+decides anything, and it is pure.
+
+| Layer | Knows about | Can it decide? |
+|---|---|---|
+| `core/` | nothing platform-specific | **yes — and only here** |
+| `probe/` | Windows, files, modules, logs | no |
+| `effect/` | Windows, ReShade config, providers | no |
+| `ui/` | ImGui | no |
+| `app/kernel` | the ports above | no — it sequences |
+
+`core/` compiles and its tests run on any machine, including a Linux CI container
+with no GPU and no Windows SDK. That is not a portability exercise; it is what
+makes 57 assertions about the product's actual rules possible.
+
+---
+
+## Evidence, and why status decays
+
+A capability's state is not stored. It is derived, every tick, from observations
+that expire.
+
+```cpp
+struct Evidence {
+  EvidenceKind kind;        // FilePresent < ModuleLoaded < ContractNegotiated
+                            //   < Configured < Liveness | Blocker
+  Confidence   confidence;  // Weak | Normal | Strong
+  Timestamp    observed_at;
+  Millis       ttl;         // Liveness: 2000 ms. Not optional.
+  BlockerCode  code;
+  std::string  detail;
+};
+```
+
+The ladder maps directly onto the status vocabulary:
+
+| Rung | Earned by | Means |
+|---|---|---|
+| `ABSENT` | nothing | not installed |
+| `INSTALLED` | the file is there, and matches its pin if it has one | it could be loaded |
+| `LOADED` | the module is mapped into this process | it is here |
+| `READY` | exports resolved, ABI matched, live control proven | it could work |
+| `ARMED` | configured for a job, inputs present | it is waiting for frames |
+| `ACTIVE` | **fresh** liveness evidence | frames are going through **now** |
+| `BLOCKED` | a blocker observation | known failure, with a reason |
+
+Two rules do the real work.
+
+**Liveness expires.** A frame counter that has not moved in two seconds is not
+evidence that frames are moving. No code has to remember to retract the claim;
+it simply stops being fresh, and the badge falls from `ACTIVE` to `ARMED` on its
+own. When delivery resumes it climbs back with no latch to clear and no restart.
+
+**Weak evidence cannot reach ACTIVE alone.** A sentence parsed out of a log we do
+not own is `Confidence::Weak`. On its own it caps the node at `ARMED`. It is only
+allowed to count once the same path also carries proof that *we configured it* —
+so a stray line in a foreign file is not a green badge, but a fresh line about a
+path we can show we set up is a real observation. The interface marks such a
+claim with `~`.
+
+```
+tests/test_capability.cpp
+  ok   active_decays_to_armed_when_liveness_goes_stale
+  ok   weak_liveness_alone_cannot_claim_active
+tests/test_planner.cpp
+  ok   a_present_file_alone_never_produces_an_active_badge
+  ok   the_badge_falls_back_on_its_own_when_delivery_stops
+```
+
+### First-party telemetry
+
+v3 adds a small shared block that SECRET EMKO's own components write into:
+monotonic counters, each with the timestamp of its last increment, plus the
+provider's status flags and a command channel back the other way (a seqlock, so
+the render thread reads it without locking).
+
+The log reader stays, demoted to one corroborating Weak source among several,
+and rewritten to read **incrementally** from the current session's attach banner
+rather than re-scanning 512 KB of tail every second.
+
+---
+
+## The dependency graph
+
+18 nodes, declared once as data, in topological order. Each edge says two things:
+what it depends on, and **how far that dependency has to climb before it stops
+holding this node back**.
+
+That second part is not a refinement, it is a correction. A passive runtime such
+as `nvngx_dlssnr.dll` is never loaded by us and never reports a frame — it is
+fully doing its job the moment it exists. Modelling it with the same rule as an
+active pipeline stage caps everything above it at `INSTALLED` forever. The tests
+caught this within minutes of the graph existing.
+
+```cpp
+Req(id)   // prerequisite: must reach READY
+File(id)  // passive artifact: presence on disk is the whole contribution
+Live(id)  // identity: this node is only as alive as that one
+Opt(id)   // enrichment: never clamps
+Any(id,g) // alternatives: the group clamps by its best member
+```
+
+The `AnyOf` group is how Neural Rendering accepts *either* motion source without
+that choice being hard-coded into the resolver. It blocks only when every
+alternative is blocked.
+
+---
+
+## Arbitration: the rules, stated once
+
+```cpp
+ArbitrationResult Arbitrate(const Intent&, const ArbitrationInput&);  // pure
+```
+
+Five booleans in, one result out, with a stable `RuleId` naming the branch that
+fired so the interface can explain itself and a bug report can name the exact
+decision.
+
+The substantive policy is unchanged from the brief:
+
+| Motion | Frames requested | Outcome |
+|---|---|---|
+| estimated (optical flow) | off | image pass runs — the normal path today |
+| estimated | 2x / 3x | **refused**; estimated motion is never a generator input |
+| geometry | off | image pass runs on the better input |
+| geometry, no shared contract | 2x / 3x | frames run, image pass suspended |
+| geometry, shared contract proven | 2x / 3x | both run together |
+
+The shared contract is a claim the input provider must make explicitly. Its
+absence means *not proven*, never *probably fine*.
+
+`tests/test_arbitration.cpp` sweeps all 32 input combinations against all 27
+intents — 864 cases — and asserts seven invariants over every one of them,
+including *generated frames never ride on estimated motion* and *the multiplier
+never exceeds what was asked for*.
+
+---
+
+## The look model
+
+Each parameter gets a three-point response curve:
+
+```
+strength 0.00  →  neutral     the value at which this parameter does nothing
+strength 1.00  →  reference   the look exactly as it was tuned
+strength 2.00  →  ceiling     the strongest value still considered sane
+```
+
+Piecewise-linear through those anchors. The tuned look sits at the middle of the
+slider, and both ends mean what the label says. Note that `neutral` is per
+parameter — `0.0` for intensity and structure, `1.0` for the tone multipliers.
+
+At strength `0.00` the planner does not merely pass neutral values down; it marks
+the pass as not granted at all, so nothing is loaded and the badge reads `OFF`
+rather than dressing up an idle pipeline as a running one.
+
+Three looks remain three parameter sets on one proven model. v3 does not claim
+three neural networks, because there are not three.
+
+---
+
+## Intent: seven fields
+
+```cpp
+struct Intent {
+  Look          look;              // Natural | Cinematic | Detail
+  float         strength;          // 0.00 – 2.00
+  FrameGen      frame_gen;         // Off | X2 | X3
+  BaseFpsMode   base_fps_mode;     // Automatic | Manual
+  int           base_fps_manual;
+  HudProtection hud;               // Automatic | Strict | Off
+  bool          auto_pause;
+};
+```
+
+Nothing in it names an implementation. The entire backend can be replaced without
+migrating a saved setting — which is the actual test of whether the abstraction
+is real.
+
+A v2 installation migrates once on first read. The three-look selection, the
+strength value (rescaled from the provider's raw intensity to the normalised
+slider) and the frame-generation choice all carry over. The dozens of v2 provider
+keys are left where they are; v3 derives them.
+
+A refused choice is **remembered, not rewritten**. Selecting 3x on a machine that
+cannot do it yet stores 3x, reports `LOCKED`, and starts working the day the
+inputs are proven.
+
+---
+
+## The kernel
+
+```cpp
+void Kernel::tick(Timestamp now) {
+  run_probes(now);                                   // read the world
+  snapshot_ = MakeSnapshot(graph_, now);             // let it expire and resolve
+  plan_     = MakePlan(intent_, snapshot_, facts_);  // decide — pure
+  apply_plan(now, force);                            // only where it differs
+  status_   = MakeStatus(snapshot_, plan_);          // project for the screen
+}
+```
+
+Every rung is re-derived from scratch. The only things that survive a tick are
+Intent (the user owns it), the evidence ledgers (they expire on their own) and
+what each effector last applied (so it can stay idempotent).
+
+There is no `g_restart_required` to get stuck, no `g_policy_synced` latch to
+forget, and no ordering between helpers that a reader has to reconstruct. The
+restart notice is recomputed every tick from the gap between what the plan needs
+and what is actually mapped into the process, so it disappears by itself.
+
+Effectors only run when the applied part of the plan actually changed — a plan
+recomputed sixty times a second must not become sixty configuration writes.
+
+### Effectors are honest about what they achieved
+
+```cpp
+enum class ApplyResult { Unchanged, Applied, Deferred, Rejected };
+```
+
+`Deferred` is a first-class outcome. Plenty of changes genuinely cannot take
+effect until the next process start, and the interface says so where the change
+was made rather than burying it on a diagnostics page.
+
+Modules are still never unloaded mid-session. They install hooks and own device
+state; tearing that down under a live frame is a worse failure than leaving an
+idle module mapped. v3 keeps v2's correct instinct here: idle it now, keep it out
+of the next start.
+
+---
+
+## The input provider: what it actually does
+
+Frame generation needs six things the game does not expose. The provider does not
+have them yet, and v3 does not pretend otherwise. What it does do:
+
+1. **Discovery that accumulates.** Shader classification (which shaders draw with
+   depth bound, which never do) is written to a candidate file and reloaded next
+   session. v2 discarded this at every exit. A classification that survives
